===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[failed]] or [[reverse]] [[climax]], ''[[particularly]]'':
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=The Two Dromios, Jim and Jouette|journal={{w|Columbia Daily Tribune}}|volume=XXXV|issue=5|location=Columbia, Mo.|date=18 September 1935|page=six|pageurl=https://www.newspapers.com/newspage/935359594/|column=1|passage=Reed, Superba of yore, made a gallant effort to summon the Promethean fire that once burned within him when it was his “listening senates to command,” but his vaunted perorations proved '''anticlimaces''' and his oratory a flop.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Konstantin Dierks|chapter=Revolution and War|title=In My Power: Letter Writing and Communications in Early America|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher={{w|University of Pennsylvania Press}}|year=2009|page=225|isbn=978-0-8122-4153-2|passage=If the siege of Boston seemed to end in '''anticlimax''', with the sudden retreat of British military forces, the siege of New York City seen from the inside, from under siege, seemed infinitely more portentous: “The Day is Come that in all Probility on which Depends the Salvation of this Countery.”}}
## {{lb|en|narratology}} An [[unsatisfying]] [[resolution]] to a [[narrative]], [[usually]] [[owing to]] a ''[[deus ex machina]]'' or [[similarly]] [[trivial]] [[resolution]] of the [[main]] [[conflict]].
## {{lb|en|rhetoric}} An [[abrupt]] [[descent]] ([[either]] [[deliberate]] or [[unintentional]]) from the [[dignity]] of the [[idea]] which the [[speaker]] or [[writer]] [[appear]]ed to be [[aiming]] for.

